{"title":"A Theory for Record Linkage","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01621459.1969.10501049","doi":"10.1080/01621459.1969.10501049","authors":["Ivan P. Fellegi","Alan B. Sunter"],"year":1969,"abstract":"Develops a probabilistic framework for deciding whether pairs of records refer to the same person, object, or event. It formalizes link, non-link, and possible-link decisions while controlling false-link and missed-link error rates, providing a foundational model for evidence combination under uncertain identity.","venue":"Journal of the American Statistical Association"}
{"title":"A Method for Calibrating False-Match Rates in Record Linkage","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01621459.1995.10476563","doi":"10.1080/01621459.1995.10476563","authors":["Thomas R. Belin","Donald B. Rubin"],"year":1995,"abstract":"Addresses how to choose a record-linkage cutoff after records have been assigned agreement weights. It models observed weights as a mixture of true-match and false-match distributions and estimates the false-match rate associated with alternative thresholds, explicitly supporting controlled identity decisions.","venue":"Journal of the American Statistical Association"}
{"title":"Cross-Document Event Coreference: Annotations, Experiments, and Observations","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W99-0201/","authors":["Amit Bagga","Breck Baldwin"],"year":1999,"abstract":"Presents early work on identifying mentions of the same event across different documents, including cross-document event-coreference annotations and experiments. It is a foundational study of event identity across document boundaries rather than ordinary within-document reference resolution.","venue":"Coreference and Its Applications"}
{"title":"Taking Email to Task: The Design and Evaluation of a Task Management Centered Email Tool","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/642611.642672","doi":"10.1145/642611.642672","authors":["Victoria Bellotti","Nicolas Ducheneaut","Mark Howard","Ian Smith"],"year":2003,"abstract":"Describes Taskmaster, an email system designed around the observation that workplace email frequently functions as a task-management environment. The interface reorganizes messages and associated resources around work tasks and was evaluated using participants' live email, providing enterprise-communication evidence about persistent task-oriented organization even though it does not establish an automated cross-thread identity benchmark.","venue":"CHI 2003"}
{"title":"Relational clustering for multi-type entity resolution","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1090193.1090195","doi":"10.1145/1090193.1090195","authors":["Indrajit Bhattacharya","Lise Getoor"],"year":2005,"abstract":"Studies entity resolution when records are connected by heterogeneous relations. The method combines attribute similarity with relational evidence and performs collective clustering, illustrating how links among surrounding objects can contribute evidence for or against an identity decision.","venue":"Workshop on Multi-Relational Data Mining"}
{"title":"A Latent Dirichlet Model for Unsupervised Entity Resolution","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611972764.5","doi":"10.1137/1.9781611972764.5","authors":["Indrajit Bhattacharya","Lise Getoor"],"year":2006,"abstract":"Presents a probabilistic unsupervised model for resolving records into latent entities. The formulation combines multiple observed attributes rather than relying on a single similarity signal and treats identity assignments as uncertain latent structure.","venue":"SIAM International Conference on Data Mining"}
{"title":"A hybrid learning system for recognizing user tasks from desktop activities and email messages","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1111449.1111473","doi":"10.1145/1111449.1111473","authors":["Jianqiang Shen","Lida Li","Thomas G. Dietterich","Jon Herlocker"],"year":2006,"abstract":"Develops a system for associating a knowledge worker's computer activities and email messages with persistent user-defined tasks. The approach combines learned task recognition with information from multiple desktop resources, making it relevant to cross-application work-instance association rather than merely classifying generic activity types.","venue":"International Conference on Intelligent User Interfaces"}
{"title":"Collective entity resolution in relational data","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1217299.1217304","doi":"10.1145/1217299.1217304","authors":["Indrajit Bhattacharya","Lise Getoor"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Introduces collective entity resolution that uses both record attributes and relationships among records and entities. Resolution decisions are made jointly so that inferred identities and relational evidence can reinforce one another, providing a transferable model for combining multiple evidence sources in identity clustering.","venue":"ACM Transactions on Knowledge Discovery from Data"}
{"title":"Online Collective Entity Resolution","url":"https://research.aimagazine.org/Library/AAAI/2007/aaai07-255.php","authors":["Indrajit Bhattacharya","Lise Getoor"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Extends collective entity resolution to an online setting where resolution is performed as new queries or records arrive. It exploits relational evidence while avoiding full recomputation, making it relevant to incremental identity assignment in a growing stream.","venue":"AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence"}
{"title":"Nonparametric Bayesian Models for Unsupervised Event Coreference Resolution","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2009/hash/185e65bc40581880c4f2c82958de8cfe-Abstract.html","authors":["Cosmin Bejan","Matthew Titsworth","Andrew Hickl","Sanda Harabagiu"],"year":2009,"abstract":"Applies nonparametric Bayesian models to unsupervised event-coreference resolution and evaluates both within-document and cross-document event identity. The models infer event clusters rather than requiring a fixed number of identities in advance.","venue":"Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems"}
{"title":"Unsupervised Event Coreference Resolution with Rich Linguistic Features","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P10-1143/","authors":["Cosmin Bejan","Sanda Harabagiu"],"year":2010,"abstract":"Studies open-domain event coreference using unsupervised models and rich linguistic features. It evaluates clustering of mentions that refer to the same events and examines evidence beyond lexical similarity, including contextual and semantic information.","venue":"ACL 2010"}
{"title":"Event correlation for process discovery from Web service interaction logs","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00778-010-0203-9","doi":"10.1007/s00778-010-0203-9","authors":["Hamid Reza Motahari-Nezhad","Regis Saint-Paul","Fabio Casati","Boualem Benatallah"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Addresses correlation of events generated by enterprise applications and Web services when traces are scattered across systems and explicit process-instance identifiers are unavailable. The method determines which events belong to the same execution instance so that business processes can be discovered from distributed logs.","venue":"The VLDB Journal"}
{"title":"Controlling false match rates in record linkage using extreme value theory","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbi.2011.02.008","doi":"10.1016/j.jbi.2011.02.008","authors":["Murat Sariyar","Andreas Borg","Klaus Pommerening"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Develops a record-linkage thresholding method aimed specifically at controlling false matches. Extreme-value modeling is used to estimate an appropriate decision threshold without requiring a labeled training set, providing transferable evidence for conservative identity assignment and false-link control.","venue":"Journal of Biomedical Informatics"}
{"title":"Semantic Relations between Events and their Time, Locations and Participants for Event Coreference Resolution","url":"https://aclanthology.org/R13-1021/","authors":["Agata Cybulska","Piek Vossen"],"year":2013,"abstract":"Examines how event components such as time, location, participants, and other semantic relations contribute to deciding whether event mentions are coreferential. The work directly investigates compatibility evidence that can establish or reject same-event identity rather than relying only on trigger similarity.","venue":"RANLP 2013"}
{"title":"Evaluation for Partial Event Coreference","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W14-2910/","doi":"10.3115/v1/W14-2910","authors":["Jun Araki","Eduard Hovy","Teruko Mitamura"],"year":2014,"abstract":"Distinguishes full event identity from partial event coreference such as subevent and membership relations. It represents partial coreference as directed hierarchical event structure and develops evaluation metrics for systems that predict those structures, explicitly demonstrating that event relations need not be a simple equivalence partition.","venue":"Second Workshop on EVENTS: Definition, Detection, Coreference, and Representation"}
{"title":"Unsupervised Event Coreference Resolution","url":"https://aclanthology.org/J14-2004/","doi":"10.1162/COLI_a_00174","authors":["Cosmin Adrian Bejan","Sanda Harabagiu"],"year":2014,"abstract":"Develops unsupervised probabilistic approaches for grouping mentions that refer to the same events. The work models event identity using rich linguistic evidence and evaluates event-coreference clustering without requiring supervised coreference labels during training.","venue":"Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"A Hierarchical Distance-dependent Bayesian Model for Event Coreference Resolution","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1504.05929","arxiv_id":"1504.05929","doi":"10.1162/tacl_a_00155","authors":["Bishan Yang","Claire Cardie","Peter Frazier"],"year":2015,"abstract":"Introduces a hierarchical distance-dependent Bayesian model for event coreference that jointly captures mention-level and cluster-level information. The model performs event clustering without fixing the number of event identities in advance and uses dependencies among mentions to improve identity decisions.","venue":"Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Event Coreference Resolution by Iteratively Unfolding Inter-dependencies among Events","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.07344","arxiv_id":"1707.07344","doi":"10.18653/v1/D17-1226","authors":["Prafulla Kumar Choubey","Ruihong Huang"],"year":2017,"abstract":"Builds event clusters iteratively while exploiting dependencies within and across event chains. It uses different classifiers for within-document and cross-document links and, after cluster merges, combines arguments from the merged events so that accumulated evidence affects later identity decisions.","venue":"EMNLP 2017"}
{"title":"Cross-Document, Cross-Language Event Coreference Annotation Using Event Hoppers","url":"https://aclanthology.org/L18-1558/","authors":["Zhiyi Song","Ann Bies","Justin Mott","Xuansong Li","Stephanie Strassel","Christopher Caruso"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Develops cross-document and cross-language event-coreference annotation using event hoppers. The annotation framework deliberately supports an inclusive conception of coreference and discusses the evidence annotators use when deciding whether event mentions across documents and languages belong to the same event hopper.","venue":"LREC 2018"}
{"title":"New Insights into Cross-Document Event Coreference: Systematic Comparison and a Simplified Approach","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.nuse-1.1/","authors":["Andres Cremisini","Mark Finlayson"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Systematically compares approaches to cross-document event coreference and analyzes which modeling choices materially affect performance. It proposes a simplified approach while retaining the task's explicit target of clustering mentions that denote the same real-world event.","venue":"Workshop on Narrative Understanding"}
{"title":"Event Coreference Resolution with their Paraphrases and Argument-aware Embeddings","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.coling-main.275/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.coling-main.275","authors":["Yutao Zeng","Xiaolong Jin","Saiping Guan","Jiafeng Guo","Xueqi Cheng"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Improves event-coreference resolution by incorporating paraphrase information and argument-aware event representations. The approach is relevant to continuity expressed with different wording because it explicitly predicts event identity while using arguments to disambiguate otherwise similar event descriptions.","venue":"COLING 2020"}
{"title":"Fast Bayesian Record Linkage With Record-Specific Disagreement Parameters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04238","arxiv_id":"2003.04238","doi":"10.1080/07350015.2021.1934478","authors":["Thomas Stringham"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Develops probabilistic record linkage with record-specific disagreement probabilities, improving decisions when common attribute values make identities ambiguous. The method is evaluated against expert-labeled matches and also provides ways to estimate true-positive rate and positive predictive value when full ground truth is unavailable.","venue":"Journal of Business & Economic Statistics"}
{"title":"Confidence Calibration for Domain Generalization under Covariate Shift","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.00742","arxiv_id":"2104.00742","authors":["Yunye Gong","Xiao Lin","Yi Yao","Thomas G. Dietterich","Ajay Divakaran","Melinda Gervasio"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Studies confidence calibration when deployment data differ from training data through covariate shift. It investigates how calibration information from multiple source domains can generalize to unseen target domains, providing transfer evidence for confidence thresholds under distribution change.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Post-Hoc Uncertainty Calibration for Domain Drift Scenarios","url":"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/html/Tomani_Post-Hoc_Uncertainty_Calibration_for_Domain_Drift_Scenarios_CVPR_2021_paper.html","authors":["Christian Tomani","Sebastian Gruber","Muhammed Ebrar Erdem","Daniel Cremers","Florian Buettner"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Investigates post-hoc confidence calibration when the test distribution drifts away from the calibration distribution. The method perturbs validation data to improve uncertainty estimates under domain shift, providing transferable evidence that IID calibration alone is insufficient for changing streams.","venue":"CVPR 2021"}
{"title":"Cross-document Event Identity via Dense Annotation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06417","arxiv_id":"2109.06417","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.conll-1.39","authors":["Adithya Pratapa","Zhengzhong Liu","Kimihiro Hasegawa","Linwei Li","Yukari Yamakawa","Shikun Zhang","Teruko Mitamura"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Directly studies the identity of textual events across different documents and argues that earlier annotation schemes insufficiently address event identity and quasi-identity. Its dense annotation workflow records coreference links together with overlapping contexts such as time, location, and participants, enabling analysis of how those evidence dimensions relate to identity judgments.","venue":"CoNLL 2021"}
{"title":"FIRLA: a Fast Incremental Record Linkage Algorithm","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbi.2022.104094","doi":"10.1016/j.jbi.2022.104094","authors":["Ahmed Soliman","Sanguthevar Rajasekaran"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Addresses incremental record linkage in which previously clustered identities must be updated as new records arrive. FIRLA reuses prior linkage output and restricts unnecessary comparisons, making it relevant to maintaining identity state incrementally rather than recomputing a complete static clustering.","venue":"Journal of Biomedical Informatics"}
{"title":"Improving Accuracy and Explainability in Event-Case Correlation via Rule Mining","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICPM57379.2022.9980684","doi":"10.1109/ICPM57379.2022.9980684","authors":["Dina Sayed Bayomie Sobh","Kate Revoredo","Claudio Di Ciccio","Jan Mendling"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Studies event correlation when business-process events lack unique case identifiers and therefore must be grouped into process instances. It introduces rule mining to improve correlation accuracy and explainability, targeting explicit event-to-case identity rather than assuming a correct case ID is already supplied.","venue":"IEEE International Conference on Process Mining"}
{"title":"Event Coreference Resolution for Contentious Politics Events","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.10123","arxiv_id":"2203.10123","authors":["Ali Hürriyetoğlu","Osman Mutlu","Fatih Beyhan","Fırat Duruşan","Ali Safaya","Reyyan Yeniterzi","Erdem Yörük"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Creates and analyzes a multilingual, multi-source event-coreference dataset for contentious-politics events and measures the contribution of event-coreference resolution to event information collection. The study highlights how separate documents can provide erroneous or partial information about events unless mentions of the same underlying event are resolved together.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Event-case correlation for process mining using probabilistic optimization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.10009","arxiv_id":"2206.10009","doi":"10.1016/j.is.2023.102167","authors":["Dina Bayomie","Claudio Di Ciccio","Jan Mendling"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Addresses business-process event logs in which case identifiers are missing and event records must be assigned to the process instances that generated them. It formulates event-to-case correlation as a probabilistic optimization problem using process-model and data constraints, directly modeling the choice among competing case identities.","venue":"Information Systems"}
{"title":"Step-by-Step Case ID Identification Based on Activity Connection for Cross-Organizational Process Mining","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2023.3284053","doi":"10.1109/ACCESS.2023.3284053","authors":["Kazuki Tajima","Bojian Du","Yoshiaki Narusue","Shinobu Saito","Yukako Iimura","Hiroyuki Morikawa"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Studies case identification for cross-organizational process mining where different organizations use independent identifier systems. It incrementally identifies probable case-ID correspondences using connections among activities, providing business-workflow evidence for correlating records that belong to the same distributed process instance.","venue":"IEEE Access"}
{"title":"CorefPrompt: Prompt-based Event Coreference Resolution by Measuring Event Type and Argument Compatibilities","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14512","arxiv_id":"2310.14512","doi":"10.18653/v1/2023.emnlp-main.954","authors":["Sheng Xu","Peifeng Li","Qiaoming Zhu"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Recasts event-coreference resolution as a prompt-based prediction problem and adds explicit auxiliary judgments for event-type compatibility and argument compatibility. These compatibility signals expose part of the evidence used to decide whether two mentions denote the same real-world event rather than relying only on opaque mention encodings.","venue":"EMNLP 2023"}
{"title":"Selective Classification Under Distribution Shifts","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.05160","arxiv_id":"2405.05160","authors":["Hengyue Liang","Le Peng","Ju Sun"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Extends selective classification, in which a model may abstain instead of making an unreliable prediction, to deployment under distribution shift. The generalized framework considers in-distribution, label-shifted or out-of-distribution, and covariate-shifted samples and evaluates confidence scores for risk-aware rejection.","venue":"Transactions on Machine Learning Research"}
{"title":"Convergence Diagnostics for Entity Resolution","url":"https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-statistics-040522-114848","doi":"10.1146/annurev-statistics-040522-114848","authors":["Serge Aleshin-Guendel","Rebecca C. Steorts"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Reviews Bayesian entity-resolution models that infer partitions of records while quantifying uncertainty and focuses on whether posterior computation has actually converged. The paper is relevant to identity-state reliability because it emphasizes uncertainty in partitions and warns that apparently stable identity assignments may reflect poor inference rather than evidence.","venue":"Annual Review of Statistics and Its Application"}
{"title":"Synergetic Event Understanding: A Collaborative Approach to Cross-Document Event Coreference Resolution with Large Language Models","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.164/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.acl-long.164","authors":["Qingkai Min","Qipeng Guo","Xiangkun Hu","Songfang Huang","Zheng Zhang","Yue Zhang"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Combines an LLM that produces comprehensive event summaries with a task-specific smaller model that learns event representations for cross-document coreference. The approach is evaluated on clustering mentions that denote the same real-world events and is intended to improve robustness to complex and diverse cross-document contexts.","venue":"ACL 2024"}
{"title":"Improving cross-document event coreference resolution by discourse coherence and structure","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2025.104085","doi":"10.1016/j.ipm.2025.104085","authors":["Xinyu Chen","Peifeng Li","Qiaoming Zhu"],"year":2025,"abstract":"Addresses the lack of coherent shared context between event mentions in different documents. It selects sentences to construct coherent cross-document context, represents that context with discourse structure, and uses the resulting global interaction evidence to resolve event identity; experiments are reported on ECB+ and GVC.","venue":"Information Processing & Management"}
{"title":"Hypergraph convolutional networks with multi-ordering relations for cross-document event coreference resolution","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.inffus.2024.102769","doi":"10.1016/j.inffus.2024.102769","authors":["Wenbin Zhao","Yuhang Zhang","Di Wu","Feng Wu","Neha Jain"],"year":2025,"abstract":"Represents events and their related elements in hypergraphs so that higher-order relationships can contribute to cross-document coreference decisions. Hypergraph convolution is used to aggregate complex relational evidence before pair scoring and clustering of mentions into same-event groups.","venue":"Information Fusion"}
{"title":"Conformal selective prediction with cost aware deferral for safe clinical triage under distribution shift","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-40637-w","doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-40637-w","authors":["Hyun Kwon","Dae-Jin Kim"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-02-20","abstract":"Combines selective prediction with conformal methods and cost-aware deferral under distribution shift. Although evaluated in clinical triage rather than workplace communication, it provides transferable evidence for allowing uncertain cases to be deferred instead of forcing an identity-like classification when confidence is unreliable.","venue":"Scientific Reports"}
===END llm_candidates.jsonl===
